Body:
We are still seeing this on current OpenCode structured-output sessions.

In our case, a client sends a prompt with:

```json
{
  "format": {
    "type": "json_schema",
    "retryCount": 2,
    "schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": ["action", "reason", "sha"]
    }
  }
}
```

The raw session history shows several assistant turns ending with `finish: "tool-calls"`, then OpenCode repeats the formatted user prompt, and the final assistant message ends with:

```json
{
  "finish": "stop",
  "error": {
    "name": "StructuredOutputError",
    "data": {
      "message": "Model did not produce structured output",
      "retries": 0
    }
  }
}
```

This matches the implementation gap described here: `retryCount` is present on the user message and defaults to 2 in the schema, but the runtime structured-output path does not appear to read it before constructing `StructuredOutputError({ retries: 0 })`.

The current source still appears to have the same behavior:

- `packages/schema/src/v1/session.ts` defines `OutputFormatJsonSchema.retryCount` with default `2`.
- `packages/opencode/src/session/prompt.ts` injects the `StructuredOutput` tool for `json_schema`.
- But when the assistant finishes without structured output, it immediately sets `StructuredOutputError` with hardcoded `retries: 0`.

PR #29632 looks like it already addressed the right root cause: preserve the original `format` on a synthetic reminder message and continue the run loop so `toolChoice: "required"` and the `StructuredOutput` tool remain active during retries, then report the actual retry count after the retry budget is exhausted.

That PR was closed by automated cleanup, not because the approach was rejected. Could it be reopened or folded into the current structured-output/session runtime work?

Thank you @markus-boesch. Between your code and a little more research, and the latest versions, I got it to work three ways: LM Studio locally via a local model, LM Studio locally via a model loaded remotely through LM Studio (same network), and LM Studio direct to that remote system (same network). This was using the OpenCode provider "LMStudio", NOT the "Custom" provider. The only catch is that I had to manually change the opencode.jsonc and make sure the API key was in the auth.json for each. In all cases I had to manually define which model I was going to use in the config. Ideally down the road Apple would write into Xcode a way to change models through this custom agent route. So, right now I tested each method, and saved individual config files that I can manually switch out. if you know a better way, let me know.  The configs that worked are below:

LMStudio Local, using GPT Oss:
```
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "model": "LMStudio/gpt-oss-20b",
  "provider": {
    "LMStudio": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "LMStudio",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1"
      },
      "models": {
        "gpt-oss-20b": {
          "options": {
            "reasoningEffort": "medium"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

LMStudio Local, running a remote model from another system on the same network also running LM Studio:

```
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "model": "LMStudio/Qwen_Qwen3.6-27B-Q4_K_L",
  "provider": {
    "LMStudio": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "LMStudio",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1"
      },
      "models": {
        "Qwen_Qwen3.6-27B-Q4_K_L": {
          "options": {
            "reasoningEffort": "medium"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```


LMStudio direct connection through network to other system running Lm Studio (no local instance of LM Studio needed):

```
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "model": "lmstudio-aw/Qwen_Qwen3.6-27B-Q4_K_L",
  "provider": {
    "lmstudio-aw": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "lmstudio-aw",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://192.168.86.3:1234/v1"
      },
      "models": {
        "Qwen_Qwen3.6-27B-Q4_K_L": {
          "options": {
            "reasoningEffort": "medium"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

it fails both ways.
It does not auto-install, and manual installs throw some kind of external_directory permission error in the log file but it never shows in the TUI.

Even running the server manually beforehand completely fails to work too.

I think it's perfectly fair to say that, at least kotlin-ls specifically, is entirely non-functional.

It's either that or the docs are insufficient and possibly completely incorrect.

---

Not even this config works:

```JSON
{
  "lsp": {
    "kotlin-ls": {
      "command": ["/home/dae/.local/share/kotlin-lsp-262.8190.0/kotlin-server-262.8190.0/bin/intellij-server", "--stdio"]
    }
  }
}
```

---

Update:

**The built-in LSP Server is completely and entirely non-functional. I had to install the AUR version (`kotlin-language-server-bin`) and set it up using the following config:

```JSON
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "lsp": {
    "kotlin-ls": {
      "disabled": true
    },
    "kotlin-community": {
      "command": ["kotlin-language-server"],
      "extensions": [".kt", ".kts"]
    }
  }
}
```

While this workaround solves **My issue**, it does not solve it for others who do not use an Arch Linux or Arch-based system.
